        "original_id": "c118ac44-c98c-531a-aad3-c5f76f1033e2",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Jackson Davis, Francis Key Hunt, Francis Scott Key\n- Places: Loudoun House, Terra Rubra, Frederick, Maryland, Georgetown, D.C., Washington, D.C.\n- Roles: architect, lawyer, author, amateur poet\n- Works: \"The Defence of Fort McHenry\", \"The Star-Spangled Banner\"\n\nFacts:\n- Francis Scott Key was born at Terra Rubra in 1779.\n- Francis Scott Key was an American lawyer, author, and amateur poet from Frederick, Maryland, and later Georgetown, D.C., near Washington, D.C.\n- Francis Scott Key wrote the lyrics to a poem first titled \"The Defence of Fort McHenry,\" which was later set to an old English gentlemen's society tune and became the United States national anthem, \"The Star-Spangled Banner.\"\n- Loudoun House was designed by New York architect Alexander Jackson Davis and built in 1851 for Francis Key Hunt.\n- Francis Key Hunt, who lived from 1817 to 1879, was named after his mother's cousin, Francis Scott Key.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
